# Keep the build context small, and keep Windows build output out of a Linux
# build. obj/ is the one that actually breaks things: project.assets.json records
# absolute host paths and the host's RID, so a copied obj/ makes `dotnet publish`
# fail inside the container with errors that name packages it never asked for.
#
# The leading **/ is load-bearing since Phase 13.1. A bare `obj/` matches only a
# top-level obj directory, which was every obj there was while src/ held one
# project. With nine, the output sits at Jobkeep.Api/obj/, Jobkeep.Modules.Ai/obj/
# and so on, sailed straight past the old pattern, and `dotnet publish` inside the
# container died in ResolvePackageAssets reading a Windows project.assets.json --
# a stack trace that names NuGet and looks nothing like "you copied obj/".
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/TestResults/

# The image does not build itself.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# IDE and editor droppings.
.vs/
*.user
*.suo
